<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1703">1703</a>–<a href="/years/1792">1792</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab was a religious scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a> whose call for a strict return to the oneness of God gave rise to the movement known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahhabism">Wahhabism</a>. Expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyayna">Uyayna</a> under pressure from the Banu Khalid emir, he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a>, where in 1744 he made a pact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-saud">Muhammad ibn Saud</a>. Their alliance founded the first Saudi state and still binds the Saudi rulers to the religious establishment.</p></div>
